image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(-3,6)$ and ${B}^{′}(5,-3),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d4f8e966818e27a0858da0aa8757cf71ebbd1ef0e0544a2b8a126810252def.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61d95268-e4f1-5580-80dc-ac1a2878fe9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5af447ff5f9726d59ad6ec00b687457bb755743d82cc801875f76557940ffaca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343068} {:answer/id #uuid "6c091fb9-bdb7-5b24-9db5-45f5ac2b76e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cdcf4d566bad1aab93fa667d2d60dbfa6e6652266b969af82917f0a29232d339.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343159} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbbf4f3-e8ae-5adb-9cd8-41a758b0cec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f8/f840b5a5922fc5b7aa8118d950cef87358755004a2b0c45b5fc3feeed1bbb054.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344032} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d79132-07f2-59b2-9c26-d1c15fc90dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ab/ab23984488fa82c6462fc5d2f4796094188e2cd17cf773071ae9389f516f1a6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344134} {:answer/id #uuid "d805beb2-9d19-5868-8834-335a96eef975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/ba458ff966132a67202469208f1daaaa98339ce22071247ca06d0f400c2e7390.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/ba458ff966132a67202469208f1daaaa98339ce22071247ca06d0f400c2e7390.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344139} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f506f6-a606-5e5c-a49f-5009432b1aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344818}] :question/id #uuid "3585fa5b-6f59-59d0-b50c-ccd2561c4797" :question/math-academy-id "q-77715" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b56d6a20b6a3e01dafa9770eb328ebaab5c14f76d87026c160c9be0d8926e74.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is shown above. Which of the following is $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}},$ the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(-3,7)$ and ${B}^{′}(6,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ba/ba458ff966132a67202469208f1daaaa98339ce22071247ca06d0f400c2e7390.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b95b62-9eac-5c79-b618-0298e1652616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15134cfea5a58d0351be842c28d087a50591d60fb6a9f8ee36bcf4b35fb950ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393760} {:answer/id #uuid "810f1d21-088a-52c7-8b34-c361d3749bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e055c227957074b5b8bb321cbc43f9c0f2796f7f7040616fe291098e9fd2dfb0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393761} {:answer/id #uuid "c597b2fb-5e49-5cfd-96c5-71b7925a4a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6e72adfafe111e75253f355d80d7d27c1a89bd2483c0db5618bee62c79f87ad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393762} {:answer/id #uuid "988f9b48-24b5-5ff5-8b0b-4a4252631376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/19dc7b72e149547ff51758dadf84f94be080a68227239e07239ce0659ade5a5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393763} {:answer/id #uuid "2ecd37a8-72a0-57e0-80f3-347934775efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/177ca7f40ef24cf2f45a15b78487700d1f0efd094c975079cf4704b46debefb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/177ca7f40ef24cf2f45a15b78487700d1f0efd094c975079cf4704b46debefb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393764} :answer-field/id #uuid "34779380-003f-5fa8-8d6a-ad93873e4a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393759}] :question/id #uuid "f9161e73-0db0-599d-b045-fa10e5d3039c" :question/math-academy-id "q-77717" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc7bebddc280debd4bde32393cabfa4a6a91a782d38e5406f374cc4eaa4ac68f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is shown above. Which of the following is $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}},$ the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ 7 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(5,7)$ and ${B}^{′}(-9,-3),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/177ca7f40ef24cf2f45a15b78487700d1f0efd094c975079cf4704b46debefb1.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136e59b4-89b9-509f-b353-97e57960580a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401146} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f7fd7a-68ed-5ca3-93c6-f651afc3e675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(5,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401147} {:answer/id #uuid "ff57b12a-81c8-5a7a-bb27-04eafe1c6040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401148} {:answer/id #uuid "5f869935-6196-536e-a49b-7e27025831a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401149} {:answer/id #uuid "bad06300-be92-5a4d-91eb-84900e7f65b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401146} :answer-field/id #uuid "51865f85-e4e0-52bf-a6e9-60f97627cefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401145}] :question/id #uuid "f9f71d03-299c-533e-bd5f-2dfe8453c281" :question/math-academy-id "q-159797" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ has endpoints at $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,2).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(2,2)$ and ${B}^{′}(-5,7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2493e3b1-7ae0-5501-828a-88fedefa33a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403894} {:answer/id #uuid "19d3cf9c-194c-52b0-a459-c6977e4d82b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403895} {:answer/id #uuid "af3e050e-2ba7-55f5-8c0e-19e72ba7d700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,1)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403896} {:answer/id #uuid "f2754c08-5978-5747-92f6-7fdb210c3ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403897} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c3b813-9270-5c10-8b7d-fc1973a88282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403894} :answer-field/id #uuid "a377066a-f0ff-5175-8fb9-ad480e6de6b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403893}] :question/id #uuid "19042bce-c915-5186-a2d2-3f28f8435edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-77699" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ has endpoints at $A(2,3)$ and $B(1,-2).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(2,1)$ and ${B}^{′}(1,-3).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Line Segment Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :topic/math-academy-id 866 :topic/title "Linear Transformations of Objects in the Plane"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060686} :activity/id #uuid "ccb8f334-2616-5c6c-bf9f-ac7f7a6bbbf5" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060941 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of linear equations and its respective **augmented matrix**:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y &=-2 \\\\ 3x-y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n$$\nM =\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\nIf we can convert the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form, then we can solve the system using back substitution.\n\nThe procedure for reducing $M$ to row echelon form using elementary row operations is called **Gaussian elimination**. To perform Gaussian elimination on a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we use the following procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Consider the entry $a_{11}=2$ of the coefficient matrix. This entry is called a **pivot element**. We want to make this entry equal to $1$. So, the first elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Consider the entry $a_{21}=3$ below the first pivot element. We want to make this entry equal to $0$. So, the second elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=R_2+(-3)R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+({\\color{blue}-3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & -10 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, consider the entry $a_{22}=-10$. This entry is the second pivot element, and we would like to make it equal to $1$. So, the third elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=\\dfrac{1}{-10}R_2\n$$\n\nand we get $M$ in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{red}-10} & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{{\\color{red}-10}}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{red}1} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=-1 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3 \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = -1 \n\t\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\tx=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left< \\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right>.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2112d3af-1fb0-5a70-ab99-59931db99930" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3151 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4af04e11-a9c8-594e-af53-b43d73f0e12d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a10cafc6-9da7-5ffc-a0c7-686a7638fbd9"} :step/id #uuid "2b9b40ab-3cb7-53b3-bb37-414d8e65412f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060942 :tutorial/content "The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique. In the previous example, after the first row operation we arrived at \n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}-10} & 5\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee4b134d-2908-5930-86bb-32537e0d8347" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3152 :tutorial/title "The Uniqueness of the Row Echelon Form Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "bafcaf30-9671-5655-95bd-e04dfa8750ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07abc1cc-5794-51a6-bad4-40565eae52be"} :step/id #uuid "381e4e89-5395-58a9-bdf6-3f814147b17f"}] :activity/title "Solving 2x2 Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059884}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059584 :question/id #uuid "0a9e6824-677a-5fae-9867-1701e7d8378f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4697" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\n  \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=0 \\\\ y &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=1$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2(1)=0 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left<  -2,1 \\right>.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a10cafc6-9da7-5ffc-a0c7-686a7638fbd9" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528072 :question-generator/id #uuid "116c0366-a3ae-546e-b005-d747f633c56d" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186059775.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "871b34d1-5